  "verdict": "the guard compares a clean model against a modified one on the SAME probes, so what its budget must beat is the spread of the difference, not of the raw reading. Across leave-one-out probe subsets the raw reading moves by up to 0.02917, while variety lost to a real perturbation moves by only 0.04167 \u2014 NOT comfortably inside the 0.05 budget. A rejection is therefore about the lever rather than about which probes were drawn",
  "why_the_raw_spread_was_the_wrong_error_bar": "The first version of this script compared the raw variety reading against the budget and reported the guard as failing. But `autotune_*` computes `base_variety - var` with both terms measured on one probe set, so probe choice cancels to first order. Measuring the level rather than the difference is the same mistake as scoring a treatment without its control.",
  "caveat": "Greedy decoding makes a repeat on identical prompts identical, so the spread here is across leave-one-out probe subsets, not across sampling. It bounds the guard's sensitivity to probe choice, which is the free parameter; it does not bound sampling noise, because the guard does not sample.",
  "the_reading_is_not_portable_across_token_counts": "Distinct-token fraction rises as the generation shortens \u2014 there is less room to repeat \u2014 so the same unchanged model reads 0.667 at 48 tokens and 0.958 at 12. The shift is several times the 0.05 budget. The budget is therefore only meaningful at a fixed token count, and a variety number quoted without its `n` says nothing. Both sides of every comparison the compiler makes use the same n, so its verdicts are sound; the number must not be read as an absolute property of a model."
}